From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:44:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] util: let mktime() work out DST in util_iso8601_to_timeval()

util_iso8601_to_timeval() passes a struct tm to mktime() that was never
initialised: strptime() fills in the date and time but not tm_isdst, so
mktime() takes whatever was on the stack. A stale 0 in summer, or a
stale positive value in winter, moves the time by an hour; valgrind
reports the uninitialised read when service_load() parses "Modified".

util_timeval_to_iso8601() writes local time (despite the "Z"), so the
string carries no DST information. Clear the struct and set tm_isdst to
-1 so mktime() decides from the time zone rules. Only the hour repeated
when DST ends stays ambiguous, as the format cannot express it.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 src/shared/util.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/shared/util.c b/src/shared/util.c
index bda2d2b..a15cbab 100644
--- a/src/shared/util.c
+++ b/src/shared/util.c
@@ -96,6 +96,8 @@ void util_iso8601_to_timeval(char *str, struct timeval *time)
 	time_t t;
 	char *p;
 
+	memset(&tm, 0, sizeof(tm));
+
 	p = strptime(str, "%FT%T", &tm);
 	if (!p)
 		return;
@@ -106,6 +108,13 @@ void util_iso8601_to_timeval(char *str, struct timeval *time)
 			return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * util_timeval_to_iso8601() writes local time, so this is local
+	 * time too, and whether DST was in effect is not in the string:
+	 * let mktime() work it out. strptime() does not set tm_isdst.
+	 */
+	tm.tm_isdst = -1;
+
 	t = mktime(&tm);
 	if (t < 0)
 		return;
